5A  Installation of lifts 5B  Installation of escalators and moving walks The significant changes incorporated in the 2016 revision included the introduction of high-speed lifts and lifts for tall buildings, the use of lifts for fire-fighting and emergency evacuation, machine room less (MRL) lifts, the introduction to destination control system, seismic resistance aspects in lifts, performance measurements for lifts and introduction of new clause on operation and maintenance of lifts.
In this revision, publication has been renamed as National Building Construction Standards (NBCS).
As a result of experience gained since the implementation of the 2016 version of this NBCS and feedback received as well as revisions of Indian Standards on which this Section was based, a need was felt to revise this Section.
The significant changes incorporated in this revision (Subsection 5A) include the following:
a) Definitions of various terms relating to energy dissipation and accumulation types, compensation means, hydraulic and electric systems, and safety mechanisms have been included to enhance clarity in performance measurement, seismic considerations, and maintenance aspects of lifts;
b) Additions have been introduced in the planning and design guidelines to address factors such as absenteeism in office buildings, provision of cafeterias and specialty facilities, population considerations for residential and hotel buildings in 4;
c) Additions have been made in the earthing provisions to elaborate on system earthing methods (TN, TT, IT), their sub-classifications, applications, and the importance of protective equipotential bonding and earth fault protection, in 6.2.3;
d) Provisions on flood sensors have been introduced for installation in lift pits to enable early detection of water ingress, alarm activation and safe shutdown of lifts, in 8.7;
e) Provisions of destination control systems (DCS) for call assignments, improving passenger flow and operational efficiency have been included, in 9.11;
f) Introduction of double-deck lift systems, with two cabins arranged one above the other, optimizing shaft use and improving efficiency in high-traffic areas and for two independent lift cabins in a single shaft solution to address up-peak and inter-floor traffic demands, in 11.7;
National Building Code Sectional Committee, CED 46 FOREWORD This NBCS (Part D/Subsection 5A) deals with the requirements for planning, design, installation, operation, maintenance and inspection of lifts (passenger lifts, goods lifts, hospital lifts, service lifts and dumb waiter) so as to ensure safe movement of people with satisfactory performance in buildings.
This Section was first published in 1970 and was subsequently revised in 1983, 2005 and 2016 as part of National Building Code of India.  This Section covers the requirements for installation and maintenance of lifts and escalators in buildings.  This Section should be read with Part F ”Fire and Life Safety‘ of this NBCS from fire safety requirements point of view.  The major changes in the first revision of 1983 were addition of outline dimensions of different types of lifts and requirements of escalators in buildings.  Emphasis was laid on coordination between the engineer/architect and the lift manufacturer to arrive at the number and position of lifts for attaining optimum efficiency in serving the building with safety.
The significant changes incorporated in the 2005 revision included addition of new clauses/recommendations on building management system; addition of new clauses on fireman‘s lift, infrared light curtain safety, and braille button for the visually impaired people and the updation of provisions as per the revised Indian Standards on lifts on which this section was based.
